"""
# Codegen: a bare `index_of` on an erased receiver is not stolen by a user method

**Category:** Codegen / method dispatch
**Bug:** doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_bare_method_receiver_type_blind_candidate_selection_2026-07-28.md

## Overview

When a receiver's static type is ERASED, the call reaches cranelift codegen as a
BARE (dot-less) `MethodCallStatic`. `compile_method_call_static` then resolves it
by NAME SUFFIX alone: any unique `Type_dot_<method>` symbol linked into the
module wins, and the receiver type is never consulted. With exactly one such
symbol there is no ambiguity to report, so `[CODEGEN-AMBIGUOUS-METHOD]` stays
silent and the wrong call is emitted with no diagnostic at all.

`index_of` was enumerated as a confirmed theft victim in the bug's 2026-07-28
census (8 binds, all stolen) but was never routed to the tag-dispatching
`rt_index_of` builtin, so an erased `text` receiver bound `.index_of(...)` to a
same-named user method and read the text as that struct.

## Why this spec shells out

The defect exists ONLY in the cranelift codegen path. `use std.spec` demotes the
whole program to the tree-walk interpreter, so an in-process `it` block cannot
reach it — an earlier version of this spec passed identically on fixed and
unfixed compilers. The fixture is therefore run as a separate `run` (JIT)
process and its stdout is asserted here.

Measured on a matched pair of seed builds differing only by the fix
(2026-08-01): unfixed prints `erased=999`, fixed prints `erased=6`.

## Syntax

```simple
val erased = "hello world".lower()   # result type erases to ANY
erased.index_of("world")             # must be 6, not Str.index_of's 999
```
"""

use std.spec

extern fn rt_process_run(cmd: text, args: [text]) -> (text, text, i64)
extern fn rt_env_get(key: text) -> text

val FIXTURE = "test/01_unit/compiler/codegen/fixtures/erased_receiver_index_of.spl"

fn compiler_bin() -> text:
    """Binary under test. `SIMPLE_SPEC_COMPILER` overrides it so a candidate
    build can be A/B'd against the deployed one.

    Deliberately NOT resolved through a `sh -c` string: `${VAR}` inside a Simple
    text literal is string interpolation, so the shell never sees it and the
    command silently degrades to an empty binary name (which made an earlier
    draft of this spec report an unrelated failure).
    """
    val override = rt_env_get("SIMPLE_SPEC_COMPILER")
    if override != "":
        return override
    return "bin/release/simple"

fn run_fixture_on_jit() -> (text, text, i64):
    """Run the fixture through the compiler's JIT `run` path."""
    rt_process_run(compiler_bin(), ["run", FIXTURE])

describe "bare index_of on an erased receiver":
    it "uses the text builtin, not a same-named user struct method":
        val result = run_fixture_on_jit()
        assert_false(result.0.contains("erased=999"))
        assert_true(result.0.contains("erased=6"))

    it "keeps resolving a typed text receiver to the builtin":
        val result = run_fixture_on_jit()
        assert_true(result.0.contains("typed=6"))

    it "still dispatches a typed user receiver to its own index_of":
        val result = run_fixture_on_jit()
        assert_true(result.0.contains("user=999"))
